<h2>Indian dehydrated food products</h2>
<p>Dehydrated onion and garlic are among the quieter success stories of Indian agricultural export — a USD 221 million line for dehydrated onion alone in 2024, moving to the USA, Brazil, Germany and food manufacturers worldwide.</p>

<p>India's advantage here is straightforward: Gujarat's Mahuva belt has the climate, the onion supply and the established dehydration capacity to produce consistently at scale.</p>

<h2>Why food manufacturers prefer dehydrated</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>No cold chain.</strong> Ships in a standard dry container. No reefer cost, no temperature risk, no cold-chain failure mode.</li>
  <li><strong>18–24 month shelf life</strong> versus weeks for fresh, which transforms inventory planning.</li>
  <li><strong>Lower effective freight cost.</strong> You aren't paying to ship water across an ocean.</li>
  <li><strong>Consistency.</strong> A specified mesh grade delivers the same product every time, which matters in formulated food manufacturing.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Specifying dehydrated products</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Form</strong> — kibbled, chopped, minced, granules or powder.</li>
  <li><strong>Mesh size</strong> — for granules and powder. Affects flow, dispersion and price.</li>
  <li><strong>Moisture maximum</strong> — typically ≤5–6%.</li>
  <li><strong>Colour</strong> — white vs red onion; and whether any browning tolerance is acceptable.</li>
  <li><strong>Microbiological specification</strong> — standard plate count, yeast and mould, salmonella and E. coli limits, per your market.</li>
  <li><strong>Pungency</strong> — for garlic and onion, where flavour strength is part of the specification.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Testing</h2>
<p>Dehydrated products are dried foods that go into formulated products, so microbiological specification matters more than for raw commodities. Testing to your specified panel can be arranged before shipment, and steam sterilisation is available for buyers requiring reduced microbial load. {{ site.complianceChecklistCta }}</p>

<h2>Loading and packing</h2>
<p>Packed in cartons with food-grade liners, or in bags depending on form and volume. Because dehydrated products are light and bulky, containers fill by volume rather than weight — expect roughly 8–12 MT in a 20ft. Use our <a href="/resources/container-loading-calculator/">loading calculator</a> for a planning estimate.</p>

